From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Grid cards share one height, and previews stay whole at a display edge

Owner report on .84: a card on the left of the row that touched the left edge
of the screen was briefly shown zoomed in or cropped, then snapped back; never
on the right. On a 1x ultra-wide with a 1.25x laptop panel to its left, a card
crossing that edge overlaps the panel, so its resource scale becomes 2. Clutter
keeps an unchanged actor's offscreen texture across frames and draws the
cached 1x texture at 1/2 (or a 2x one at double) until the actor is redrawn,
which for a still window lasted as long as the card straddled the edge.
RoundedPreview now measures the scale its texture was actually rendered at
(texture size over paint volume) and, when it differs from the scale Clutter
draws at, adds a transform node to draw it at its real size. Nothing goes
through a JS float return, which is what broke the earlier vfunc attempt.

Grid's row also changes design. Every card now has one height for the
display: 46% of the height the row may use, no taller than lets 2.5 16:10
cards across, and at least 240px, so it does not depend on which windows are
open. A card is its window's shape at that height, and the preview fits inside
it by aspect ratio, scaled down and never up, never cropped, centred when it
does not fill the card, so no preview is ever zoomed and every title sits on
one line. A window shorter than the row keeps its own size; a window whose
card would pass 80% of the display's width gets a card that wide, and its
preview is scaled to that width and so is shorter than the card. Such cards
show a quiet surface behind the preview. Cards scale their preview uniformly,
so it is not stretched while travelling either.

Generated against the Shell .84 tree (0123 applied) for release .85.
